 1100px) {\n    .hero-inner, .main { max-width: calc(100% - 70px); }\n    .hero-copy { width: 65%; }\n    h1 { font-size: 38px; }\n    .authors { font-size: 22px; }\n    .placeholder { right: -100px; transform: scale(.8); transform-origin: top right; }\n    .main { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 70px; }\n    .references { padding-left: 35px; padding-right: 35px; }\n  }\n  @media (max-width: 650px) {\n    .hero { height: 500px; }\n    .hero-inner, .main { max-width: calc(100% - 36px); }\n    .hero-copy { top: 45px; width: 100%; }\n    h1 { font-size: 30px; }\n    .authors { font-size: 18px; }\n    .placeholder { opacity: .45; top: 190px; right: -75px; transform: scale(.62); }\n    p, .result-text { font-size: 18px; }\n    h2 { font-size: 27px; }\n    table { font-size: 16px; }\n    th { font-size: 16px; }\n    .references { padding-left: 18px; padding-right: 18px; }\n    .references li { font-size: 16px; }\n  }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <header class=\"hero\">\n    <div class=\"hero-inner\">\n      <div class=\"hero-copy\">\n        <h1>Investigating the Impact of Magnetic<br>Fields on the Superconductivity of<br>High-Temperature Ceramics</h1>\n        <div class=\"authors\">Dr. Alice Green, Dr. Patrick Green, Leslie Thomas, Diego Ruiz</div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"placeholder\">\n        <div class=\"glow\"></div>\n        <div class=\"base\"></div>\n        <div class=\"upper\">\n          <span class=\"hole h1\"></span>\n          <span class=\"hole h2\"></span>\n          <span class=\"hole h3\"></span>\n          <span class=\"hole h4\"></span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </header>\n\n  <main class=\"main\">\n    <div class=\"left\">\n      <section>\n        <h2>Introduction</h2>\n        <p>Superconductivity is a state of zero electrical resistance occurring at low temperatures in certain materials. High-temperature superconductors (HTS), such as yttrium barium copper oxide (YBCO), exhibit superconductivity above the boiling point of liquid nitrogen. This study explores how varying magnetic fields affect the superconducting properties of YBCO ceramics, providing insight into their practical applications in energy transmission and magnetic levitation.</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2>Methodology</h2>\n        <p>We synthesized YBCO ceramic samples with varying compositions of yttrium (Y), barium (Ba), copper (Cu), and oxygen (O). Using a cryostat, we cooled the samples to temperatures ranging from 77 K to 90 K. Magnetic fields ranging from 0 to 5 Tesla were applied perpendicularly and parallel to the sample surface. The critical temperature was determined by measuring the resistivity of the samples as a function of temperature in the presence of the magnetic field. The data were analyzed to find the values of Tc and Hc2</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section class=\"results\">\n        <h2>Results</h2>\n        <div class=\"result-item\">\n          <div class=\"result-icon\"></div>\n          <div class=\"result-text\">The superconducting transition temperature decreased as the magnetic field increased</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"result-item\">\n          <div class=\"result-icon magnet\"></div>\n          <div class=\"result-text\">The rate of decrease was more pronounced for higher magnetic field strengths</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"result-item\">\n          <div class=\"result-icon search\"></div>\n          <div class=\"result-text\">The upper critical field for YBCO was found to be 4.8 Tesla when the field was applied</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"result-item\">\n          <div class=\"result-icon arrows\"></div>\n          <div class=\"result-text\">When the magnetic field was applied perpendicular to the surface, Hc2 increased to 5.1 Tesla</div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n    </div>\n\n    <div class=\"right\">\n      <section>\n        <h2>Critical Temperature (Tc) vs. Magnetic<br>Field Strength for Perpendicular and<br>Parallel Orientations</h2>\n        <div class=\"table-wrap\">\n          <table>\n            <thead>\n              <tr>\n                <th>Field Strength<br>(T)</th>\n                <th>Tc (Parallel)<br>(K)</th>\n                <th>Tc (Perpendicular)<br>(K)</th>\n              </tr>\n            </thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>0</td><td>90</td><td>90.0</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1</td><td>88</td><td>87.5</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2</td><td>86</td><td>85.0</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>3</td><td>84</td><td>82.5</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>4</td><td>82</td><td>80.0</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <section class=\"discussion\">\n        <h2>Discussion</h2>\n        <p>Our results demonstrate that magnetic fields significantly alter the superconducting properties of YBCO ceramics, with field orientation and strength playing critical roles in Tc and Hc2. The anisotropic behavior observed in the samples suggests that further research is needed to optimize YBCO materials for practical applications in magnetic environments, such as in high-speed magnetic levitation trains and power transmission lines.</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section class=\"conclusion\">\n        <h2>Conclusion</h2>\n        <p>Magnetic field-induced suppression of superconductivity in high-temperature ceramic superconductors can be mitigated by adjusting the composition and the orientation of the magnetic field relative to the sample. Future studies should focus on enhancing flux pinning mechanisms to extend the functional temperature range of YBCO superconductors in magnetic fields.</p>\n      </section>\n    </div>\n  </main>\n\n  <footer class=\"references\">\n    <h2>References</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Zhang, X., &amp; Wang, L. (2021). Magnetic field effects on the critical temperature of high-temperature superconducting ceramics. Journal of Superconductivity and Novel Magnetism, 34(5), 455-467. https://doi.org/10.1234/jsnm.2021.0506</li>\n      <li>Patel, R. S., &amp; Kumar, V. (2020). Exploring the role of magnetic fields in enhancing the superconductivity of ceramic materials. Materials Science and Engineering Reports, 29(8), 1123-1136. https://doi.org/10.5678/mserep.2020.0804</li>\n    </ul>\n  </footer>\n</body>\n</html>"
